Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	| Pin-close error skips Shared cleanup | Finish independent retirement, preserve primary/secondary errors, and preserve surviving-reader leases/snapshots. A real Linux OS I/O fault reproduces on `eb01` and passes the correction; a peer writer progresses before the surviving reader ends. |
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	| Shared parent final-checkpoint contract | Narrow documentation to its historical best-effort behavior; parent-only fault tests retain WAL and recover committed indexed data. Pin/child errors still propagate. |
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	| Raw-close callback dependency; ordinary callback self-wait | Reject fresh work behind close and same-handle callback lock self-waits; preserve unrelated operations and idle-handle handoff. Intermediate scope-release and refused-close/native-release defects have explicit guards; restore context before admission handoff and refuse raw callback disposal before mutating ownership. Raw disposal still cannot bound an arbitrary application callback that never returns. |
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	| Cancellable mutex polling | Windows uses blocking `WaitAny` with a scoped cancellation event/registration; Unix retains bounded waits because named-mutex `WaitAny` is unsupported there. Uncontended admission allocates neither. Cancellation, release and owner-death controls pass; source inspection alone establishes no performance attribution. |
